= The health system and societal costs of osteoarthritis in New Zealand: Methodology and results of the process for funding an optimal matching model
#smallcaps[Published]\ 05 July 2023

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Wilson R, Abbott JH, Pryymachenko Y. The health system and societal costs of osteoarthritis in New Zealand: Methodology and results of the process for funding an optimal matching model. FigShare, 05 July 2023. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.22661551.v3\")[10.6084/m9.figshare.22661551.v3]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
*Background*
Diagnosis and first-line management of osteoarthritis (OA)
typically occur in primary care, with surgical management (joint replacement surgery)
reserved for those with more severe late-stage disease. Despite this, there is
a lack of data available on the outcomes of OA managed in primary care (in contrast
to a wealth of data on surgical outcomes). This report describes the methodology
for a matched cohort analysis to estimate the outcomes of OA diagnosed in primary
care settings and presents results of the process to find optimal matching models
for this analysis. (Analysis of outcomes, using the matched cohorts identified
here, will be reported subsequently.)

*Method*
A cohort of individuals enrolled
with one regional primary healthcare organisation in New Zealand (NZ) was linked
with the Integrated Data Infrastructure (IDI), a linked research database maintained
by Statistics NZ containing data from government administrative datasets, Statistics
New Zealand surveys and data collections, and non-governmental organisations.
OA cases were defined as individuals having a first recorded onset of OA in the
primary healthcare records in the period January 2009#sym.dash.en;December 2010;
potential controls were drawn from all individuals enrolled with the primary healthcare
organisation with no recorded diagnosis of OA during or prior to December 2010.
Data on healthcare utilisation and costs, socioeconomic status, and other personal
characteristics were extracted from existing datasets within the IDI. Separate
cohorts were identified with (1) hip OA, (2) knee OA, and (3) either hip or knee
OA. For each cohort, matching models were developed to identify, from the pool
of potential controls without the corresponding OA diagnosis, a matched sample
who were as similar are possible, across a large selection of baseline covariates,
to those with an OA diagnosis. Optimal models were chosen based on diagnostic
balance statistics (standardised mean difference, variance ratio), graphical inspection
of the covariate distributions in the matched case and control groups, and any
exclusion of cases by the matching models. Separate subgroup analyses were conducted
for cohorts stratified by sex, ethnicity, and socioeconomic deprivation.

*Results*
Very good quality of matching was achieved for all three OA diagnosis cohorts
and for all subgroups. Excellent balance was achieved across all 40 measured baseline
covariates (standardised mean difference less than 0.1 (in absolute value), variance
ratio between 0.8 and 1.25, and minimal observable difference in variable density
plots) in the optimal models for all cohort and subgroups, with few unmatched
observations (1–2% in each cohort, with the exception of the (small) Māori subgroup
at 6–8%).

*Conclusion*
The matched cohorts identified here will be used to
estimate the healthcare, personal, and socioeconomic burden of OA diagnosed and
managed in primary care.

